  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor and RAP analysis shows a highly amplified \nridge from the Arklatex northward into the Great Lakes. Meanwhile, a \nnegatively tilted trough is lifting northeastward across the \nnorthern Rockies. Some high clouds are filtering into the forecast \narea between these two features. The ridge reaches its maximum \namplitude over the forecast area through this evening, with 500 mb \nheights in the upper 570s (dam), at the 99th percentile of \nclimatology. The trough continues moving across the northern Plains \nand into Ontario by Thursday, with its associated surface reflection \nremaining well north of the forecast area. Pressure gradient between \nthis feature and a high anchored over the central Appalachians \nwill continue to result in a feed of unseasonably warm (see \nclimate section below) and rather dry air from the southwest. \nBreezy conditions are also expected - forecast sounding suggest \nsomewhat limited mixing depths today, but 25-30 mph gusts are \nstill forecast. Thursday, mixing depths will increase leading to\na breezy day across most of the forecast area, 30-40 mph gusts \nbeing common. Increased winds from the NBM both of these days.\n\nWinds diminish for Friday as a high pressure ridge briefly builds \nin. Meanwhile, a deep, positively tilted trough shifts across the \nintermountain west. A piece of energy ejecting from this trough will \nresult in a cyclone developing and again tracking well north of the \nforecast area, resulting in another unseasonably warm day as strong \nsoutherly winds develop. Rain showers will become likely from west \nto east Saturday into Saturday night as the southern portion of the \nwestern US trough lifts into the Plains. Timing and precip amounts \nappear to be tied to whether and how quickly this portion of the \ntrough lifts northeastward and assumes a negative tilt, thus \nspinning up a wave along the passing front. Precip amounts to not \nlook to be significant, but there is about a 20% chance of amounts \nexceeding an inch by Sunday evening, which would help alleviate \nongoing drought conditions. Cluster analysis shows little chance of \ncold air interacting with the deeper moisture, so the synoptic \nportion of the system is forecast to be primarily if not entirely \nliquid p-types Saturday into Saturday night (and possibly into \nSunday if the slower solutions pan out). Some lingering lake effect \nsnow on northerly winds can be expected Sunday night into Monday, \nbut model consensus favors high pressure quickly building in early \nin the week, so the lake effect is not expected to be significant at \nthis time. Overall NBM probs of > 2\" of snow Sunday night into \nMonday haven't changed since yesterday at 25% or less. Temps are \nforecast to return to seasonable values early next week with highs \nin the upper 20s to mid-30s.",
